# Terraform working files. The repository's root .gitignore covers Python, logs and
# .env; none of its rules match these, and two of them are the reason this file
# exists rather than a preference.
#
# *.tfstate, and any backup of one, must never be committed: state is a clear-text
# record of every attribute of every resource, and the day someone adds a secret
# version resource it is a clear-text record of the secret too (versions.tf). State
# for this workspace lives in the S3 backend; a local state file here means someone
# ran `terraform apply` without `init` against the backend, which is itself worth
# noticing.
#
# terraform.tfvars is ignored because it is the file a person fills in. It holds no
# secret by design (terraform.tfvars.example says so twice), but it is per-deployment
# and the example is what the repository carries.
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
terraform.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars
crash.log

# NOT ignored, deliberately: .terraform.lock.hcl. The provider lock is how two
# people and CI get the same provider version, which is the point of pinning it.
!.terraform.lock.hcl
